{
  "analysis": "I'm in a fresh container shell at /app as root. Nothing has been done yet. I need to (1) locate or obtain alpine.iso, (2) verify qemu and supporting automation tools (expect/python) are available, and (3) plan how to boot the ISO and configure an ssh server inside it with root password 'password123' reachable on host port 2222.",
  "plan": "First explore the environment: check the working directory contents, look for any existing alpine.iso, confirm qemu availability, and check for automation helpers (expect/python3) plus the OS/distro. This tells me what I have to work with before designing the boot+configure workflow.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "ls -la /app\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "ls -la\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "which qemu-system-x86_64 qemu-system-i386 expect python3\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "cat /etc/os-release\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "find / -iname 'alpine*.iso' 2>/dev/null\n", "duration": 5.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Environment: Debian 11, qemu-system-x86_64/i386, expect, and python3 are available. /app contains alpine.iso (~957MB) and a tiny alpine-disk.qcow2. No KVM certainty yet. I need to figure out how to get a scriptable serial console into the live Alpine ISO (does its bootloader enable ttyS0?) and whether openssh is available offline. Let me inspect.",
  "plan": "1) Check for /dev/kvm and extraction tools. 2) Loop-mount the ISO to read its syslinux/isolinux config and see if serial console (ttyS0) is enabled by default, and check whether an openssh .apk is bundled. This determines my boot approach (plain -cdrom vs extracted kernel + append console=ttyS0).",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "ls -l /dev/kvm 2>&1; echo ---\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "which 7z xorriso isoinfo bsdtar genisoimage 2>&1; echo ---\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "mkdir -p /mnt/iso && mount -o loop,ro /app/alpine.iso /mnt/iso 2>&1; echo MOUNT_EXIT=$?\n", "duration": 1.0},
    {"keystrokes": "ls -la /mnt/iso 2>&1 | head -40\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "find /mnt/iso -iname '*.cfg' 2>/dev/null; echo ---; find /mnt/iso -iname '*syslinux*' 2>/dev/null | head\n", "duration": 1.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: no /dev/kvm (so TCG software emulation, slower), no ISO extraction tools, and mount -o loop is blocked. But qemu, expect, and python3 are present. The alpine.iso is in /app. Rather than fight extraction, the decisive question is whether the Alpine live ISO enables a serial console (ttyS0) by default — if it does, I can boot with -serial and get an auto-login root shell directly, making configuration trivial. Let me test that empirically.",
  "plan": "Boot the ISO in the background with a serial port writing to a log file and the monitor on a unix socket (no KVM, so TCG). Wait and inspect /tmp/serial.log. If Alpine outputs boot messages to ttyS0 and presents a login/shell, serial console works and I can automate the rest with expect. If the log is empty, I'll need to enable serial another way.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "cd /app\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "nohup qemu-system-x86_64 -m 512 -smp 2 -cdrom /app/alpine.iso -netdev user,id=n0,hostfwd=tcp::2222-:22 -device e1000,netdev=n0 -display none -serial file:/tmp/serial.log -monitor unix:/tmp/qmon.sock,server,nowait >/tmp/qemu.out 2>&1 &\n", "duration": 1.0},
    {"keystrokes": "sleep 8; echo '--- qemu.out ---'; cat /tmp/qemu.out; echo '--- serial.log ---'; cat /tmp/serial.log\n", "duration": 9.0}
  ]
}

